1
00:00:00,253 --> 00:00:04,083
Portion helps people who run earning equipment, like solar canopies,

2
00:00:04,083 --> 00:00:06,924
share revenue with the people who backed them.

3
00:00:06,935 --> 00:00:09,923
It records the agreed terms, splits each deposit,

4
00:00:09,923 --> 00:00:12,796
and lets each participant claim their part.

5
00:00:13,586 --> 00:00:18,877
Without a shared record, operators coordinate terms, payments and allocations by hand,

6
00:00:18,877 --> 00:00:21,772
and participants take the numbers on trust.

7
00:00:23,151 --> 00:00:26,681
Portion doesn't create revenue or guarantee returns,

8
00:00:26,681 --> 00:00:29,396
and can't prove all income was reported.

9
00:00:29,396 --> 00:00:33,108
Participants receive only money actually deposited.

10
00:00:33,384 --> 00:00:36,834
This is the real deployment, on the Portaldot testnet.

11
00:00:36,844 --> 00:00:40,224
Native assets represent the shares and test pUSD.

12
00:00:40,224 --> 00:00:44,087
A fractionalized NFT links the deed to the share supply.

13
00:00:44,087 --> 00:00:49,510
Hash-checked terms are stored, and a Solidity contract does the accounting.

14
00:00:49,521 --> 00:00:56,662
These recorded transactions show 1 test pUSD deposited, and 0.995 claimed.

15
00:00:56,673 --> 00:00:59,021
One development account played both roles,

16
00:00:59,021 --> 00:01:01,787
and none of this verifies physical equipment.

17
00:01:02,051 --> 00:01:04,954
To show separate roles, we enter the sandbox:

18
00:01:04,954 --> 00:01:10,429
Core v2 in the browser, with fictional roles and pretend funds, not on Portaldot.

19
00:01:11,818 --> 00:01:15,705
Buyer A already has a sandbox allocation of 59.7,

20
00:01:15,705 --> 00:01:19,684
from Riverside Solar Canopy, a fictional asset.

21
00:01:21,972 --> 00:01:24,193
The operator pays in 30.

22
00:01:24,193 --> 00:01:28,238
After a small fee, 29.85 goes to holders.

23
00:01:31,029 --> 00:01:37,446
Buyer A holds two thirds of the staked shares, so the deposit adds 19.9.

24
00:01:37,446 --> 00:01:39,300
79.6 is now ready.

25
00:01:39,317 --> 00:01:43,588
Buyer A claims, and the balance rises by exactly that amount.

26
00:01:43,588 --> 00:01:47,268
This is deposited money, not a guaranteed return.

27
00:01:50,447 --> 00:01:53,468
Core v2 adds something the live contract lacks:

28
00:01:53,468 --> 00:01:55,710
purchase money stays in escrow.

29
00:01:55,725 --> 00:01:59,303
The operator requests the next tranche, and tries to release it.

30
00:02:00,588 --> 00:02:04,337
The contract refuses. The named reviewer has not approved.

31
00:02:06,163 --> 00:02:07,488
The reviewer approves.

32
00:02:07,488 --> 00:02:11,462
After a one-week challenge window, shown as a simulated time skip,

33
00:02:11,462 --> 00:02:13,218
the release goes through.

34
00:02:16,913 --> 00:02:18,917
Core v2 is local and unaudited.

35
00:02:18,917 --> 00:02:23,499
On the live contract, purchase money goes straight to the operator.

36
00:02:23,751 --> 00:02:28,189
For operators, the aim is less allocation admin and clearer records.

37
00:02:28,189 --> 00:02:31,702
Participants see deposits and what they can claim.

38
00:02:31,713 --> 00:02:36,593
Deployed fees are 1% of sales and 0.5% of deposits, in test tokens.

39
00:02:36,593 --> 00:02:39,069
That would not sustain a business,

40
00:02:39,069 --> 00:02:43,398
so a paid operator service is proposed, but unvalidated.

41
00:02:43,653 --> 00:02:47,247
Core v1 is live on testnet, Core v2 runs locally,

42
00:02:47,247 --> 00:02:49,770
and there are no customers yet.

43
00:02:49,780 --> 00:02:53,601
Next, we test this with a real operator, in a pilot.
